---
import authzData from '../data/authz.json';
import PrivilegeRoles from '../demos/surfaces/PrivilegeRoles.tsx';
import DocsLayout from '../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';

const headings = [
  { depth: 2, slug: 'model', text: 'Users, groups, roles, grants' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'privileges', text: 'Privilege matrix' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'roles', text: 'Role configuration' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'audit', text: 'Audit log' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'oidc', text: 'OIDC' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'settings', text: 'Settings and secrets' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'keys', text: 'API keys and sessions' },
];
const roles = authzData.roles;
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Administration"
  description="Users, groups, built-in roles, project grants, the privilege matrix, audit, OIDC, settings, and API keys."
  headings={headings}
  wide
>
  <header class="docs-page-head">
    <p class="docs-eyebrow">Identity</p>
    <h1>Administration</h1>
    <p class="docs-lede">
      Authorization is privilege-based. Roles are named bundles of privileges; grants add a role's
      privileges inside one project. This matrix is generated from
      <code>packages/contract/src/authz.ts</code>.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="docs-prose">
    <h2 id="model">Users, groups, roles, grants</h2>
    <p>
      A user has global roles and any number of group memberships. Groups carry global roles, so
      membership grants the same privileges. Project grants attach a project role to a user or group
      for one project. Global role names are
      <code>{authzData.globalRoles.join(', ')}</code>; project grants may use
      <code>{authzData.projectRoles.join(', ')}</code>. <code>owner</code> exists only on project
      grants, and the project's creator holds it implicitly.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every check is the union of global privileges and the matching project grant's privileges. An
      API key cannot exceed its owner's current privileges.
    </p>

    <h2 id="privileges">Privilege matrix</h2>
    <p>
      {authzData.privileges.length} privileges in {new Set(authzData.privileges.map((p) => p.split('.')[0] ?? p)).size} resource groups.
      The <strong>Admin</strong> role holds every privilege by definition.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="docs-table-scroll">
    <table aria-label="Privileges per built-in role">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Privilege</th>
          {roles.map((role) => <th>{role.name}</th>)}
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          authzData.privileges.map((privilege) => (
            <tr>
              <td><code>{privilege}</code></td>
              {roles.map((role) => (
                <td class="tabular" style="text-align:center;">
                  {role.privileges.includes(privilege) ? '●' : '·'}
                </td>
              ))}
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="docs-prose">
    <p class="docs-lede" style="font-size:12px;">
      ● granted · · not granted. Project grants use the same bundles with the same matrix.
    </p>

    <h2 id="roles">Role configuration</h2>
    <p>
      The role editor uses the same matrix as checkboxes, grouped by resource prefix and rendered
      read-only for a built-in role. Switch roles below: the grid is the real
      <code>PrivilegeGrid</code> from <code>apps/web/src/components</code>, fed the built-in role
      bundles from <code>packages/contract/src/authz.ts</code>. Custom roles and API-key scopes use
      the same component with an editable <code>onToggle</code>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="docs-demo">
    <div class="docs-demo__head">
      <span class="docs-demo__name">PrivilegeGrid</span>
      <span class="docs-demo__note">Read-only, grouped by resource; the role buttons are docs chrome around the real component.</span>
    </div>
    <div class="docs-demo__stage">
      <PrivilegeRoles client:visible roles={roles} />
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="docs-prose">
    <h2 id="audit">Audit log</h2>
    <p>
      Every mutation is written with the user, IP, action, resource, and a redacted diff. Secrets and
      password fields are redacted before the row is stored. <code>audit.read</code> is held by
      administrators and auditors; the UI exposes filters by user, action, and time. Audit rows are
      append-only through the API — there is no delete procedure.
    </p>

    <h2 id="oidc">OIDC</h2>
    <p>
      Providers are managed with <code>auth.oidc.*</code> and require <code>settings.write</code>.
      Discovery uses <code>openid-client</code>; PKCE, state, and nonce are stored in a signed
      10-minute cookie. Claims map to groups, and optional auto-provisioning creates users on first
      login. Issuers must use https, except loopback issuers used for local mock providers;
      <code>NB_ALLOW_INSECURE_OIDC</code> exists only for that case.
    </p>

    <h2 id="settings">Settings and secrets</h2>
    <p>
      Global settings are typed key/value rows read with <code>settings.get</code> and written with
      <code>settings.update</code>. Secrets (IOU license files, credentials, tokens) are stored with
      AES-GCM using <code>NB_SECRET_KEY</code> and referenced by id; they are never returned in
      plaintext through list calls. Changing <code>NB_SECRET_KEY</code> without re-encrypting makes
      existing secrets unreadable.
    </p>

    <h2 id="keys">API keys and sessions</h2>
    <p>
      Sessions are random 32-byte tokens stored as SHA-256, delivered as the HttpOnly
      <code>nb_session</code> cookie, and revocable per session. API keys use the
      <code>nbk_</code> prefix, are stored hashed, and are scoped to a privilege subset. The
      plaintext is returned exactly once at creation. Prefer one key per integration and revoke it
      when the integration ends; scopes are intersected with the owner's privileges at check time.
    </p>
  </div>
</DocsLayout>
